# Input graph for BD2KOnFHIR__fhirtordf__fhirtordf_rdfsupport_rdfcompare.py__rdf_compare_split_primary_subjects
@prefix ex: <http://example.org/> .

# Several solutions: three URIRef subjects.  With `g1` restored as an alias
# of the function's own argument (see meta.json), these ARE what
# `primary_subjects` returns -- the BNode branch below never contributes,
# see the next comment.
ex:Alice a ex:Person .
ex:Bob ex:knows ex:Alice .
ex:Carol ex:has _:child .

# Neighbourhood that must NOT match: ex:Person only ever appears as an
# OBJECT here, never as a subject -- it must not leak into the result.

# BNode subjects, present to exercise the per-item `m{}` navigation
# (`subject_predicates`, the region's one genuine multi-step lookup) over
# more than one candidate.  Neither ever reaches the output: the region's
# own "has no incoming predicate" check is dead code (it wraps a generator
# in a length-1 list, so `len([...]) == 0` never holds -- see meta.json),
# so it makes no difference that `_:orphanish` truly has no incoming edge
# while `_:child` does (via `ex:Carol ex:has _:child`).
_:orphanish ex:note "no incoming edge at all" .
_:child ex:note "has an incoming edge from ex:Carol" .
